A||||16||sully5||Grand Am brakes||||5||
Z||000000||sully5||02-29-2000||07:47 AM||||OK, here is another one for yall-have any of you heard or tried using Grand-Am front brakes as a replacement for the Fiero rear brakes? I found an article on the Fiero Focus On-Line site that describes this. The guy who wrote it said that the parts bolt right up, but you can't use the e-brake cables (mine are shot anyway), and you can't re-use the mounting tab on the flex hose||||reg||5
Z||000001||batboy||02-29-2000||03:31 PM||||Yes, I've done the Grand Am brake swap on the front and rear of my 85 SE (Batmobile). If you use the front rotors and calipers from a late 80's Beretta or Grand Am on the rear of the Fiero, it is a bolt in except for three minor items. 1) The mounting boss on the caliper bracket must be ground down a tiny bit in two places. 2) The brake dust shield must be trimmed a little and bent slightly out of the way. 3) The flexible brake hose can be used. Carefully pry the crimp loose on the hold down bracket (located in the middle of the hose) and slide the bracket to one side or the other until the hose fits again. Bolt it back down and check to make sure there is enough slack in the hose for suspension travel. I don't recommend doing the rear without doing the front though. Most of the braking is on the front, if you only improve the back, then the rear will lock up first during hard braking and this will put you into a skid, maybe into the ditch.||||reg||1
Z||000002||sully5||03-01-2000||12:01 AM||||batboy- thanks for the input. I am going to pull the fronts tomorrow night and see what kind of shape they are in. If those calipers are OK and the rotors can be turned, I am just going to replace the rear calipers(with stock ones) and go on with it. There is so much more that needs to be done to the car I am going to try to put the up grade off for now.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Uaana||03-01-2000||03:13 AM||||Just wanted to add my 2c worth..<BR>Another added benefit to doing the upgrade<BR>(did it on my 87GT) is the parts cost about<BR>half as much for replacement.. And if you can<BR>get a hook up on the machining of the old rotors.. this will pay for itself in about 2 years..||||reg||1
Z||000004||la9||03-01-2000||01:25 PM||||I just bought a set of calipers for the front of the fiero and stuck them on the rear. They bolt right up with no mods. I think the factory braking ratio is supposed to be 80/20 so it shouldn't affect the braking enough to cause any problems. Also they are 31.99each at advance auto or 59.99each at autozone. Hope this helps. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||03-01-2000||04:51 PM||||Be careful as I think they're different calipers.  (Someone correct me if I'm wrong.)||||reg||1
