Z||000012||batboy||03-06-2000||11:43 AM||||Dan-o did an excellent job machining my old rotors down into hubs. Seems like more people are doing the Grand Am brake swap nowadays, so I'm sure it can be done cheaper than previously. Of course, I can buy the same computer cheaper this year  than last year too. It doesn't matter, I'm happy, the Batmobile is happy, the hubs were perfectly done, and the brakes work great. I also had the peace of mind knowing Dan-o was a Fiero lover and had done this mod before.||||reg||1
Z||000013||BBBAD GT||03-07-2000||09:14 AM||||Could you list out the parts for the Grand Am brake upgrade, and where you got them from with prices?  I'm trying to decide if this is the route I want to take, and I'm getting some prices on some web auto parts (always seems the local parts store wants an arm and leg for everything)stores.  Thanks.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000014||batboy||03-07-2000||10:42 AM||||The total cost for the whole 1988 Grand Am to 1985 Fiero brake swap, front and rear, is approximately $400 which includes American made rotors and OEM rebuilt calipers which cost more money. I compared the Chinese rotors with the American rotors and decided to spend the extra $10 more each, there was a visible difference in quality. I also replaced all the wheel bearings, flexible brake hoses, and bought premium semi-metallic brake pads. A guy could do this a lot cheaper if he really wanted to. Lastly, I'm keeping my old calipers as backups for my other Fieros, so I didn't have any core returns ($10 per caliper). If I would have bought all of the same Fiero brake parts and rebuilt it all back to original, the cost probably would of been over $500.<P>I bought everything at two different local chain auto parts stores: O'Reilly's and Parts America (which has since been bought out by Advance Auto). Some of the imported rotors are real cheap, in price and in quality. I'm planning to change the master cylinder over to a S-10 unit this spring too. The Fiero brake fluid tank fits right on the S-10 M/C which has a bigger bore and more volume, it's suppose to work well with the Grand Am calipers. Hope this helps. Go look at the link I listed in a previous post too for more details.||||reg||1
Z||000015||BBBAD GT||03-07-2000||11:57 AM||||BB:  What yr brake stuff did you get?  I've seen on different articles two different years of the Grand Am/Baretta brakes to get, 88 or 91.  I'd like to know the differences between the two, if any.  Thanks.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000016||batboy||03-07-2000||12:13 PM||||From the info I have, you can use the front brake calipers and rotors from any 1985-1991 Beretta or any 1985-1990 Grand Am. There is something goofy or different about the 1991 Grand Am calipers, but I don't remember what. Someone else in an earlier post mentioned the 1991 Beretta calipers had improved sliders, but I don't know anything about that. To stay consistent, I bought all 1988 Grand Am parts and everything worked fine for me.||||reg||1
